  "explanation": {
    "easy": {
      "heading": "Warming Up With A Morning Brew",
      "body": "I started my morning with the easy grid and a fresh cup of coffee. The first thing that caught my eye were the empty spots at (0,0) and (2,3). Even though they are empty, they are still part of a domino, which helps narrow down the choices. I noticed the Equals region at (3,3) and (4,3) right away. I decided to use the [6,6] domino there, which felt like a solid anchor. \n\nNext, I looked at the Sum of 2 region at (1,2) and (1,3). I realized that if I put the [3,1] domino across (3,1) and (3,2), it would satisfy the Less than 2 constraint because the 1 pip lands perfectly on (3,2). This left the [1,4] domino to handle the Sum of 2 area, placing the 1 pip at (1,2). The rest of the pieces, like the [5,5] and [6,1], fell into the larger Equals region and the remaining gaps quite naturally once those first few were set."
    },
    "medium": {
      "heading": "Navigating The Midday Maze",
      "body": "The medium puzzle today felt like a bit of a treasure hunt. I spotted a Sum of 0 constraint at cell (0,2) and knew that was my starting point. Since only a zero can satisfy that, I looked for a domino with a 0. I ended up placing the [1,2] domino so that the 0 side rested on (0,2). This immediately helped me figure out the Sum of 3 region right below it at (1,2) and (2,2).\n\nThings got a little more crowded in the middle of the board. I had to manage several regions that all wanted a sum of 3. I found that placing the [3,1] domino vertically at (3,1) and (2,1) satisfied the Less than 3 constraint perfectly. The Equals region at (3,4) and (4,4) was the final piece of the puzzle. I used the [2,4] domino there, and it was such a relief to see all the numbers line up with their neighbors without any leftovers."
    },
    "hard": {
      "heading": "A Deep Dive Into The Domino Deep End",
      "body": "Today's hard puzzle was a real brain teaser, especially that massive Equals region covering five different cells like (2,1) and (4,1). I spent several minutes just staring at the Greater than 10 constraint at (2,0) and (3,0). I knew I needed two very high numbers there, so I saved my [6,4] and [5,5] dominoes to see where they would fit best. I eventually used a combination that put high pips in that zone to clear the 10-point hurdle.\n\nThe breakthrough happened when I tackled the top right corner. The Sum of 10 at (0,4) and (0,5) and the Sum of 7 at (1,4) and (1,5) were like two interlocking gears. Once I placed the [0,5] and [2,6] dominoes in that area, the rest of the board started to make sense. I had one small moment of panic near the bottom with the Greater than 0 constraint at (5,1), but placing the [5,1] domino there solved it instantly. It was a tough one, but finishing it felt amazing."
    },
    "tips": "Always look for regions with a target of zero or very low numbers first, as they have fewer possible domino combinations. If you see a very large Equals region, try to find a domino that fits one end and see if those same pips are available for the other cells in that group. Don't be afraid to work from the corners inward, as the edges often have the most restrictive constraints.",
    "learned": "I learned today that Equals regions spanning across multiple dominoes are actually great for narrowing down your high-value pips like 5s and 6s. I also noticed that the empty cells are often the key to unlocking the dominoes next to them, acting as a silent hint for the rest of the board.",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What does an empty cell mean in the NYT Pips puzzle?",
        "answer": "An empty cell is a specific constraint where that part of the domino must have zero pips. It effectively acts as a target sum of zero for that single cell."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you solve Equals regions in Pips?",
        "answer": "In an Equals region, every single cell within that colored area must contain the exact same number of pips, regardless of which domino they belong to."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I reuse dominoes in the same Pips puzzle?",
        "answer": "No, each domino provided in the list for the day can only be used once in the grid to complete the solution."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the best strategy for high sum constraints in Pips?",
        "answer": "For regions that require a sum greater than 10, look for your dominoes with 5s and 6s immediately, as these are the only ones that can reach such a high total."
